        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lafayette-la-princesse-de-cleves-676df60b4dfe3de4\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Monsieur de Clèves directly presents his desperate love as a real emotional state that explains why jealousy has deprived him of reason. The imperfect « aimais » is embedded under « vous aviez donc oublié que », but it remains a forceful avowal or reminder of love. His marital status is separately invoked, not undertaken through the love clause. Despite the scene's violence and agitation, the clause functions argumentatively as an emotionally intense assertion rather than independently as reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The surrounding speech is highly impassioned, but intensity and loss of composure do not by themselves establish E; the highlighted clause remains principally truth-conditional.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A first-person love avowal embedded in Monsieur de Clèves's direct rhetorical question to his wife: « Vous aviez donc oublié que je vous aimais éperdument…? »\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Clèves uses the avowal to explain and reproach: his desperate love, combined with being her husband, accounts for the extremity of his jealousy. The subsequent contradictions—adoration and hatred, offense and apology—frame him as emotionally destabilized, but do not alter the highlighted clause's primary force as an assertion of his loving state. The statement does not itself promise fidelity or renew a marital commitment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« Vous aviez donc oublié que je vous aimais éperdument et que j'étais votre mari? »\",\"supports\":\"The embedded clause explicitly presents Clèves's desperate love as a fact his wife should remember, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« L'un des deux peut porter aux extrémités: que ne peuvent point les deux ensemble? »\",\"supports\":\"Clèves treats love and marital status as two explanatory causes of his extreme reaction; this supports an argumentative avowal rather than a commitment enacted by the love words.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« Je n'ai que des sentiments violents et incertains dont je ne suis pas le maître » followed by « Je vous adore, je vous hais »\",\"supports\":\"The speech is affectively violent and unstable, but these surrounding descriptions supply contextual intensity rather than independent evidence that the highlighted words themselves are a reflex-like discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as the affirmative explicit-love form « je vous aimais » in Madame de La Fayette's La Princesse de Clèves.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the highlighted event is an explicit first-person love predication in an embedded construction.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the clause's avowal of a loving emotional state. The jealousy, reproach, marital appeal, and emotional turmoil are adequately treated as context; no residual core force requires P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
